# Strangerly backend settings.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every value below has a safe default in config/env.go, so the backend also
# runs with no .env at all. This file is the template for both cases: the
# defaults are what local development wants, and the lines marked "production"
# are what a deployed service wants.
#
# .env is git-ignored, and so is every .env.* variant except this template. No
# real credential belongs in a committed file.
#
# Two files, one shape:
#   .env        what the deployed service runs with (ALLOWED_ORIGINS, cookies)
#   .env.local  optional, read first and wins, for a development machine only.
#               A deployed host must not have one.
#
# One rule matters more than the rest: the systemd unit loads this file with
# EnvironmentFile=, so every line must be KEY=value. A value pasted from a
# dashboard (JSON, YAML, a JavaScript snippet) stops the service from starting.
# Comments go on their own line — systemd does not strip a comment that trails
# a value.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Where this runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# development | production. Production logs JSON and is the only mode that
# should ever face the internet.
ENVIRONMENT=development
# production:
# ENVIRONMENT=production

# HTTP listen address. Keep the default and let the reverse proxy terminate TLS.
LISTEN_ADDR=:8080

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The browser app: CORS and the WebSocket Origin allowlist
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The exact origin the app is served from: scheme included, no trailing slash.
# Comma separate several. This one list gates CORS *and* the signaling WebSocket
# handshake, so a mismatch here is the difference between a working call and a
# socket that never opens. The startup log prints what it accepted
# ("allowed_origins=[...]"), and a refused handshake logs the origin it saw.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
# production:
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://strangerly.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The anonymous session cookie
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

COOKIE_NAME=strg_session

# true in production, so the cookie only ever travels over HTTPS. Keep false
# only for plain-HTTP local work; with true and no HTTPS the browser stores
# nothing and every request answers 401 "no session".
COOKIE_SECURE=false
# production:
# COOKIE_SECURE=true

# Leave empty for a host-only cookie, which is the safest choice when the app
# and the API share a host. Set it (".example.com") only when the API has to be
# reachable from several subdomains.
# COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Timers. A bare number is read as seconds ("5"); Go durations ("2h") work.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How long an idle anonymous session survives. Slides forward on activity.
SESSION_TTL=2h

# How long a waiting queue entry survives if the visitor never comes back.
QUEUE_TTL=2m

# Grace period before a participant that stopped answering loses the call.
MATCH_IDLE_TTL=20s

# Hard cap on a single call, so a forgotten tab cannot hold one open forever.
MAX_MATCH_DURATION=1h

# The server-enforced Skip window. Clients cannot shorten or bypass it.
SKIP_LOCK_SECONDS=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. ICE: how two browsers find each other
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# STUN lets each browser learn its own public address. Enough for two people on
# the same Wi-Fi or behind friendly home routers. Comma separate several.
STUN_URLS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302

# TURN relays the media when the two networks cannot reach each other: mobile
# data behind carrier NAT, corporate firewalls, guest Wi-Fi with client
# isolation. Without it those calls never connect at all — the call screen
# opens, both sides stay on "Connecting", and the peer connection is declared
# failed about fifteen seconds later. This is the most common production
# surprise in this project, so add TURN before real users arrive.
#
# TURN_URLS is a comma separated list, and listing several transports is
# deliberate: UDP for the good case, TCP for networks that drop UDP, and TLS on
# 443 for networks that allow nothing else. All of them share one credential.
# TURN_URLS=turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=udp,turn:turn.example.com:3478?transport=tcp,turns:turn.example.com:443?transport=tcp

# Form A, preferred: a shared secret. The backend mints a short-lived username
# and credential per request, so no static credential ever reaches a browser or
# the frontend bundle. This is what coturn's use-auth-secret expects.
# TURN_SHARED_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-secret
# TURN_CREDENTIAL_TTL=1h

# Form B: a hosted relay that only offers one static username/credential pair.
# It is handed to every visitor, so treat it as public, and check the plan's
# quota: a relay that stops relaying looks exactly like the NAT failure above.
# TURN_USERNAME=replace-me
# TURN_CREDENTIAL=replace-me

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Request budgets, per client
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

RATE_LIMIT_SESSION_PER_MINUTE=20
RATE_LIMIT_JOIN_PER_MINUTE=60
RATE_LIMIT_SIGNAL_PER_SECOND=60
RATE_LIMIT_REPORT_PER_10MIN=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Logging
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# debug | info | warn | error. "debug" traces every relayed offer, answer and
# ICE candidate, which is what a call that never connects needs.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Behind a reverse proxy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Empty means gin uses the socket peer address, and with a proxy on the same
# host that is 127.0.0.1 for everybody — every visitor would then share one rate
# limit bucket. Set it to the proxy's address (comma separated) so the real
# client address is read from X-Forwarded-For.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1
